Yes—generally this is permitted from the Singapore side, provided the personnel remain physically outside Singapore and remain employees of the overseas entity. Singapore has no general “inbound cross-border staff-leasing” licence for that arrangement.

The main distinction is between:

a foreign entity providing services using its own employees; and

placing individuals into employment with, or effectively supplying employees to, the Singapore client.


The first is normally permissible; the second can attract employment-agency and employment-status issues.

Issue	Likely Singapore position

Work passes	None required while personnel work entirely outside Singapore. MOM’s requirement applies to foreigners intending to work in Singapore. (MOM)
Employment-agency licence	Normally not required where the overseas entity remains the employer. MOM expressly exempts “outsourcing your own staff to your customers’ premises or offices.” (MOM)
Pure labour supply	Singapore restricts deploying Singapore work-pass holders merely to supplement a client’s workforce, but that rule should not apply to personnel who remain abroad. (MOM)
Withholding tax	Ordinarily none where the services are performed wholly abroad. IRAS specifically confirms that electronic provision from overseas without staff entering Singapore is rendered outside Singapore and attracts no WHT. (IRAS)
GST	Imported-services reverse charge may apply to a Singapore business that cannot fully recover input GST. A non-GST-registered client may also affect the overseas supplier’s GST-registration analysis. (IRAS)
CPF	Singapore CPF is ordinarily not mandatory for employees working overseas. (CPF Board)
Data protection	Giving overseas personnel access to Singapore personal data should be treated as an overseas data transfer. The Singapore organisation must ensure comparable protection, normally through appropriate contractual and security measures. (PDPC)


The arrangement becomes materially riskier where:

the Singapore client hires, dismisses, disciplines or appraises the individuals;

the individuals are integrated into the client’s reporting structure indefinitely;

remuneration is simply passed through salary-by-salary, with no service responsibility assumed by the overseas entity;

the Singapore entity is actually matching workers with the client for employment;

personnel begin making working visits to Singapore;

the foreign entity maintains a Singapore office or a Singapore agent habitually concludes contracts for it; or

the work is itself regulated—such as financial services, legal services, healthcare, regulated engineering or government/security work.


For foreign-company registration, section 368 of the Companies Act requires registration before a foreign company establishes a place of business or commences carrying on business in Singapore. Merely serving a Singapore customer remotely, without personnel, premises or contracting authority here, would ordinarily present a low registration/PE risk, but repeated direct contracting through a Singapore group entity or agent should be examined separately. (Companies Act 1967, s 368)

The safer structure is:

1. The overseas entity remains the legal employer and retains hiring, dismissal, pay, leave and performance-management responsibility.


2. The contract is a services or subcontracting agreement with an identifiable scope, responsibility for deliverables and appropriate substitution rights.


3. Client instructions concern project requirements and priorities, while an overseas manager retains employment supervision.


4. The client contract expressly permits subcontracting/offshoring.


5. An intercompany services agreement covers IP ownership, confidentiality, data processing, security and liability.


6. Cross-charges between related entities are arm’s-length and documented; IRAS applies the arm’s-length principle to related-party services. (IRAS)


7. Each worker’s home jurisdiction is checked for local temporary-agency/staff-leasing licensing, employment, payroll, social-security, works-council and GDPR requirements. EU/EEA status matters much more on this origin-country side than under Singapore manpower law.


8. Any proposed visit to Singapore is separately cleared before travel.



So, on your stated facts—existing staff, remaining at their normal overseas location, employed and paid there, servicing a Singapore client remotely—the Singapore answer is yes, generally allowed, subject principally to tax, data protection, contractual, origin-country and regulated-activity checks.